<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.8.1: Evaluating Expressions Using Mental Math</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02213</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.8.1: Evaluating Expressions Using Mental Math</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a0cd6f2b-73ea-4c96-9beb-4c7c8a81308f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<para>Finding a product mentally can be done by changing the way you look at a multiplication problem.</para>
<para>For example, \(9 \cdot 39\) can be thought of as \(9 \cdot (40 − 1)\). For some people, this might be easier to calculate mentally since you can imagine the solution as \(360 − 9\), or 351. This method leverages the distributive property of multiplication over addition.</para>
<para>For the following questions, find each product mentally. Then, explain how you changed the way you looked at each multiplication problem to use the distributive property.</para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>(\(9 \cdot 11\))</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the product and your explanation.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>99</para>
    <para>For example, \(9 \cdot 11\) can be thought of as \(9 \cdot (10 + 1)\). This simplifies to \(90 + 9\), or 99.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>(\(19 \cdot 21\))</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the product and your explanation.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>399</para>
    <para>For example, \(19 \cdot 21\) can be thought of as \(19 \cdot (20 + 1)\). This simplifies to \(380 + 19\), or 399.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>(\(99 \cdot 101\))</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the product and your explanation.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>9999</para>
    <para>For example, \(99 \cdot 101\) can be thought of as \(99 \cdot (100 + 1)\). This simplifies to \(9900 + 99\), or 9999.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>(\(109 \cdot 101\))</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the product and your explanation.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>11,009</para>
    <para>For example, \(109 \cdot 101\) can be thought of as \(109 \cdot (100 + 1)\). This simplifies to \(10,900 + 109\), or 11,009.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
